            <div xml:id="d1031">

                <pb facs="0913" n="906" sameAs="frus:frus1937v03/pg906"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">693.002/423: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Counselor of Embassy in China</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Lockhart</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-12-17T15:00:00+08:00">December 17, 1937—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received December 17—9:30 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>831. Embassy’s 828, December 16, 4 p.m.<anchor n="21" xml:id="d1031-f1"/>
                    According to a Japanese press report the customs at Tientsin was taken over
                    yesterday afternoon by the new regime and the five barred flag raised over the
                    customshouse. However, senior member of the Tientsin customs staff is reported
                    as stating that so far as he was aware no change has taken place in regard to
                    jurisdiction over the customs. The Japanese report may be based on Wang Keh
                    Min’s statement contained in the above-mentioned telegram.</p>
                <p>Repeated to the Ambassador and Tokyo.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lockhart</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
